WEBVTT

00:00:00.632 --> 00:00:24.216
<v Riley>Hey there, good to have you. It's Tuesday, so we're in the Agriculture and Ranching stretch. Today we've got Quesnel River Pulp's curtailment now running indefinitely, and the Tŝilhqot'in framework that could shape how projects get reviewed. Plus, Spain ordering olive mills to hold back twelve percent of their oil. Then we dig into who gets the shifts when the mill comes back. Stick around.

00:00:37.691 --> 00:00:59.603
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Tuesday, November 10th, 2026, and today's theme is Working Lands &amp; Industry. We're broadcasting from the Cariboo, on the traditional and unceded territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:01:00.292 --> 00:01:30.140
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today's stories are about money and rules landing on working land, and who gets served first when they do. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's overcast and around 2 degrees, with a high of 10 and light drizzle through the day. There's a three-in-four chance of precipitation, about a millimetre. 100 Mile House is around 3 with partly cloudy skies. Williams Lake is around 4 and overcast, Quesnel is around 4 with light drizzle, and Hanceville is around 2 under mainly clear skies.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.414
<v Riley>We'll start the news close to home.

00:01:33.814 --> 00:02:10.860
<v Casey>The first stories today are from our own backyard, and the rest is farm news from farther away. Millar Western's curtailment at Quesnel River Pulp, which we covered yesterday with its original dates, has now become indefinite. Karl Meyers, the company's executive chair and CEO, wrote to employees on November 9th and called it a "very difficult decision." The reasons he gave are market conditions and uncertain economic fibre supply. The effluent system keeps running for twelve months, or until the company reaches an acceptable alternative. The rest of the operation winds down over several weeks, and Meyers says that leaves the mill set up for a possible restart.

00:02:12.175 --> 00:02:53.885
<v Riley>So the mill goes idle, and the effluent system runs for another year. Next is Williams Lake, where All-Ways Travels won the Ken Wilson Chamber Business of the Year award, judge's choice, at the Williams Lake and District Chamber's business excellence awards on October 24th. Owner MJ Vigeant and her daughter, manager Tanya Slack, kept the agency open through the pandemic. Travel restrictions stopped their growth plans and left them unable to keep staff on. Vigeant kept helping residents reach family members abroad. She recalled, "I've never had so many tears." The award recognizes leadership, business performance, and commitment to the local business community.

00:02:55.064 --> 00:03:26.944
<v Casey>In Quesnel, the Community Foundation has a $50,000 challenge running from October 15th to December 15th. If the community reaches that first $50,000, an anonymous donor adds another $50,000. Sandra Lewis, the foundation's president, says it began as a legacy fund from hosting the 2000 BC Winter Games, with matching money from the city and the Vancouver Foundation. She puts the fund at about 7.1 million dollars now. You can donate online, by e-transfer, or by cheque to the foundation's office.

00:03:28.088 --> 00:04:05.118
<v Riley>Grants come from the interest, so the principal has to keep growing. On to Ottawa. The 2026 federal budget, tabled Thursday, November 5th, includes the forestry package we noted yesterday. The money covers fibre supply, facility modernization, and higher-value products, and it sits on top of $2.9 billion announced for the sector since August 2025. B.C. leaders welcomed it. The Canadian Forest Owners want private forest land counted in the fibre supply plan. The Williams Lake Tribune put high deficits in the background of the whole budget, and we had that figure on Saturday.

00:04:06.282 --> 00:04:38.400
<v Casey>Private woodlot owners want a seat in a fibre plan that's still being written. Quesnel also held its own swearing-in, following the Williams Lake ceremony we covered Sunday. The procession on Tuesday, November 3rd, was led by Lhtako Dene Nation Elder Bryant Paul, who opened with a prayer for the new council. Lhtako Dene Nation Chief Clifford Lebrun said there are many issues the nations, the city, and the Cariboo Regional District can work on together in a difficult time. Nazko First Nation Chief Leah Stump said leadership comes with a responsibility to listen.

00:04:39.748 --> 00:05:05.116
<v Riley>Down south, well over a dozen holiday markets are planned across the South Cariboo this winter. The first big one is the 100 Mile House Christmas Craft Fair on Saturday, November 14th, from 9:30 in the morning to 4 in the afternoon at the 100 Mile House Community Hall. Dozens of local vendors will be set up, and the organizer has added a second fair on Saturday, December 12th.

00:05:06.463 --> 00:05:25.671
<v Casey>Nearby, the Interlakes Volunteer Fire Department now has a fully outfitted 2026 Ram 3500 pickup, set up as a squad truck and mobile command centre. Chief Poul Beer says Cariboo Regional District tax dollars fund the department, and a society raises money for everything beyond that. Large community donations paid for this truck.

00:05:27.095 --> 00:06:10.029
<v Riley>That's a small operation getting capital it couldn't generate on its own, and the next story has the same shape at a much larger scale. The U.S. Department of Agriculture is moving forward with nearly $4 million for ten Kansas meat processors to expand local processing for livestock producers. That's part of a $57.4 million round covering 147 awards in 40 states. The largest Kansas grant is $2 million to Plainview Beef Company in Peabody, to renovate and equip a facility with cold storage and packaging. Hat Creek Butchery's daily harvest capacity is projected to rise 200 percent. The USDA says the money can begin flowing after a congressional notification period.

00:06:11.454 --> 00:06:35.302
<v Casey>I'd like to know how far a Cariboo rancher hauls to the nearest processor. Next, Small Farm Canada's planting guide covers when to plant and harvest carrots, beets, parsnips, radishes, turnips, rutabagas, potatoes, and garlic. Root crops handle cool conditions, and many store well through winter. The guide treats planting dates as a starting point, since a grower in one region can work the soil weeks before a grower farther north.

00:06:36.639 --> 00:07:09.653
<v Riley>In Washington State, the USDA confirmed bird flu in 40 dairy herds between October 7th and October 30th, and thirty-three of them were in Yakima County. Rick Naerbout, executive director of the Washington State Dairy Federation, says containment is hard because cattle farms can't be sealed as tightly as poultry barns. In early October the federation told farmers to follow their biosecurity plans and alert veterinarians if milk production drops. Washington officials say infected cows generally recover within four to six weeks.

00:07:10.872 --> 00:07:50.910
<v Casey>Biosecurity is a daily cost on a dairy, and it doesn't stop when milk sales do. Dairy technology keeps spreading. New Zealand company Herd-i now covers 145,000 cows on 130 farms with its AI monitoring system. A Mid Canterbury farmer says the number of cows flagged as potentially lame fell from 400 in the first year to 30 by the third season. In Canada, Milk Moovement says it reached 9 percent savings on transport by the end of September, against a target of 3.5 percent. And in the Australian state of Victoria, Agriculture Victoria is testing drones and AI to monitor exclusion fencing and detect dingoes. That pilot runs through June 2027.

00:07:52.137 --> 00:08:32.447
<v Riley>Fewer flags could mean healthier cows. It could also mean someone tuned the threshold. In Sweden, farm-data platforms are linking farm records, crop-protection information, and field-work logs. Agronod, a data-sharing platform, has formed a farmer council to advise on how the data gets used, and its link to a farm-management program lets users reuse records. A separate system puts small tags on implements so field tasks are logged automatically, with an estimated service life of about five years. Council member Lars-Johan Merin says use should stay voluntary, and that regulatory-compliance data should be kept separate from commercial market data.

00:08:33.764 --> 00:08:59.396
<v Casey>Spain has ordered olive mills to hold back 12 percent of their output, its first compulsory olive-oil withdrawal. Growers say extra-virgin oil is fetching about three euros a kilo, while production costs on many farms are close to five. Mills get no payment for storage. The rule runs until September 2027, and the ministry can revise the percentage from December 31st. Bottlers and refiners say the order distorts supply and plan to challenge it.

00:09:00.582 --> 00:09:35.924
<v Riley>Farms in New Zealand, Poland, and Vietnam are adding income alongside the main crop. A 35-hectare celery farm in New Zealand added sunflower fields and a butterfly house after returns on green vegetables fell. A Polish pumpkin-seed business buys growers' harvests and processes the seeds, and it handled about 5,000 tonnes last year. In Vietnam, a family interplanted durian among coffee on about three hectares and reports more than a billion dong a year from it. New Zealand co-owner Jasmine Franklin says, "Diversification isn't necessarily an easy way to make money."

00:09:37.263 --> 00:10:09.461
<v Casey>In West Africa, Guinea has banned illegal artisanal gold mining in Siguiri and expelled hundreds of foreign miners. Many have crossed into Mali, and some have reached Bamako. Burkina Faso says it is organizing transport and reception sites for returnees, has mobilized about 100 buses, and received no advance notice from Guinea. Mali's health ministry says more than 130 patients were treated in 72 hours after it activated an emergency hospital plan. Guinea's foreign minister says the operation protects the country's land and natural resources.

00:10:10.796 --> 00:10:37.524
<v Riley>The people with the least leverage are the ones who walked across the border. And on Prince Edward Island, Cleanfarms is running free collection events from November 16th to 27th for old agricultural pesticides and livestock and equine medications. Collection runs from 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. at several sites. A farmer with a cabinet full of obsolete products can get rid of them there at no charge before the end of the month.

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:50.204
<v Casey>That wraps the news. Closer to home, Ottawa's forestry money is promised, Quesnel's mill is idle, and who sets the rules for projects in between is still open. First, a local group working on farm questions.

00:10:51.604 --> 00:11:17.620
<v Riley>The Cariboo Agricultural Research Alliance does applied agricultural research for farmers and ranchers here. That's how they describe themselves, and it's a plain description of a useful job. Research only matters once it reaches someone on a tractor or at a calving pen in March. If you farm or ranch here and have a problem that needs testing on Cariboo ground, look them up at cariboo-agricultural-research dot c-a.

00:11:18.204 --> 00:11:23.476
<v Casey>Research counts once it shows up in the ranch yard, and I'm glad a local group is aiming for that.

00:11:23.805 --> 00:11:27.253
<v Riley>Now to the forestry money and the review rules, in the Deep Dive.

00:11:28.653 --> 00:11:57.669
<v Riley>Picture a mill crew in Quesnel. Some of them stayed through every curtailment, and one skilled millwright moved to town last spring. When shifts and money come back, who should get them first? The forestry money and the review rules both decide that, and they run on different clocks. The $6.5 billion we noted yesterday doesn't start flowing until 2027-28. Meanwhile Bill C-39 is being pushed through to fast-track major project review.

00:11:58.313 --> 00:12:17.361
<v Casey>And yesterday the Tŝilhqot'in National Government released its own impact assessment framework. It tied the release directly to Ottawa's rush on C-39. The bill is still in front of a special committee in the House, and the Senate is pre-studying it at the same time. It's a 246-page omnibus. Nobody knows its final shape.

00:12:18.004 --> 00:12:38.476
<v Riley>The framework is the most concrete thing on the table, so we'll start there. It's titled "Our Laws for Our Lands." It sets out values, guiding principles, decision-making criteria, and a process for projects that touch nen, the Nation's land, water, and resources. A proponent can read the test before filing instead of finding it out halfway through.

00:12:39.049 --> 00:12:47.041
<v Casey>Readable isn't binding. The framework is still a draft under review by the Nation's leadership. And Ottawa hasn't said how its own review will treat it.

00:12:47.697 --> 00:13:14.385
<v Riley>Ottawa's own case for Bill C-39 is that major projects wait five years or more for all their federal decisions. The proposed fix is to coordinate impact assessments with other federal reviews and with federal consultation of Indigenous groups. A Nation that has already published its criteria has done part of that coordination itself. The federal review would start with the Nation's questions already on the record.

00:13:14.939 --> 00:13:22.291
<v Casey>Or it starts with two sets of questions, and the proponent chooses which to answer first. Who decides when the two reviews disagree?

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:33.800
<v Riley>Nobody has answered that yet, and it's the right question. A disagreement between two published tests is still easier to see and argue over than one settled quietly partway through a review.

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:38.506
<v Casey>Easier to see from the outside. The people carrying the risk are on the outside.

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:52.112
<v Riley>Then look at that risk. The Impact Assessment Agency is supposed to finish its review before a project moves forward. Critics say the bill could let Ottawa issue permits for preliminary construction before the review ends.

00:13:52.675 --> 00:14:23.673
<v Casey>Preliminary construction is still construction. Ground gets broken while the review is running, and if the review goes sideways, the ground stays broken. The Assembly of First Nations calls the bill fundamentally flawed and wants it withdrawn. West Coast Environmental Law told a committee it will bring in a dismantling of environmental law. Indigenous Climate Action wants UNDRIP and free, prior and informed consent written in as binding. These are the groups the consultation is supposed to reach, and they're asking for the bill to be pulled or rewritten.

00:14:24.249 --> 00:14:43.249
<v Riley>Ottawa says projects will be built faster without lowering standards. The Narwhal reports that consultation feedback showed widespread agreement that reform should not come at the expense of environmental protection or Indigenous rights. So the government and its critics agree on the outcome and fight over the method.

00:14:43.778 --> 00:14:54.282
<v Casey>Agreeing on the goal is the easy part. "Coordinated consultation" is a promise about scheduling. The Crown's duty to consult belongs to each Nation, and a shared calendar doesn't discharge it.

00:14:54.965 --> 00:15:12.029
<v Riley>That's why the Tŝilhqot'in framework works as a test case. A Nation-built standard exists now, on paper, dated November 9th. If Ottawa's coordinated process can slot that standard in and still move faster, the government's claim holds up. If it can't, we'll see the failure in writing.

00:15:12.598 --> 00:15:26.862
<v Casey>Or the framework shows the opposite. A Nation spent the effort to build its own parallel review because it doesn't trust the federal one to protect its rights. That's what a vote of no confidence looks like when it's written by people who expect to be ignored.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:43.704
<v Riley>Some of it is distrust, sure. Some of it is ambition. A Nation that writes its own test is also positioned to say yes on its own terms, and that includes economic partnerships. Waiting five years for Ottawa costs those partnerships too, along with the industry jobs.

00:15:44.255 --> 00:15:56.191
<v Casey>Then look at the mill side. The framework covers Tŝilhqot'in land and resources. Quesnel River Pulp sits in Dakelh territory, and Lhtako Dene led the swearing-in procession there. The comparison only stretches so far.

00:15:56.731 --> 00:16:03.939
<v Riley>Agreed on the boundary. I'm offering a template for how a Nation writes its own tests. I don't think it fixes Quesnel.

00:16:04.544 --> 00:16:25.952
<v Casey>Then take the money's clock. The mill went idle this week. The forestry money starts in 2027-28 and runs over six years. Kim Haakstad at COFI, the BC Council of Forest Industries, welcomed it as coming at a "critical time" and in the same breath said more action is needed. Meanwhile the sector is facing combined U.S. tariffs and duties of around 35 percent right now.

00:16:26.466 --> 00:16:39.506
<v Riley>A six-year program is the kind of horizon a mill needs before it commits to retooling. Nobody rebuilds a planer line on a one-year grant. And the industry's own welcome tells you they read it as a real commitment.

00:16:40.093 --> 00:16:47.421
<v Casey>A real commitment that starts after the crew has scattered. Productivity money doesn't help a town whose workforce left in the gap.

00:16:47.940 --> 00:17:14.596
<v Riley>So what working towns need now is certainty about the rules, because the cash comes later. And some of the money goes straight at Quesnel's stated problem. The budget sets aside $500 million over six years for fibre supply, for provinces, territories, and First Nations to improve forest management and the technology behind supply chains. Millar Western blamed fibre for the curtailment, and fibre has its own line in the budget.

00:17:15.169 --> 00:17:32.961
<v Casey>$500 million out of $6.5 billion, across the whole country, and nobody has said how it gets divided between provinces. Who gets the rest? The budget categories are fibre supply, modernizing facilities, and higher-value products. Modernizing a facility means the company that already holds the facility.

00:17:33.615 --> 00:17:44.367
<v Riley>Modernization money for an existing mill is how the jobs get kept. A sawmill that can't upgrade can't compete for the next contract, and the crew is gone before the market turns.

00:17:45.038 --> 00:17:56.974
<v Casey>And while the company decides whether to upgrade, the workers and the town carry the risk. On the effluent bill we mentioned up top, the company pays to keep a closed mill on standby whether or not a restart happens.

00:17:57.501 --> 00:18:10.925
<v Riley>It pays because standby is cheaper than a cold rebuild. If the mill were dismantled, a restart would be a new project, with a new permit path and new consultation. Keeping the effluent system running keeps the asset alive.

00:18:11.452 --> 00:18:19.500
<v Casey>Cheaper for the company. A year of idle time lands on the people who lost their shifts. The company keeps the asset, and the crew keeps the rent.

00:18:20.096 --> 00:18:25.936
<v Riley>The asset is also what any restart depends on, and that restart is where those shifts come back.

00:18:26.284 --> 00:18:28.692
<v Casey>"Potential restart" has no owner and no date.

00:18:29.308 --> 00:18:42.436
<v Riley>A clear process gives it a timeline. A restart that has to pass a known Nation test has one, even a long one. A fast-tracked approval that gets challenged in court leaves the same mill idle with more lawyers involved.

00:18:42.749 --> 00:18:45.813
<v Casey>Nothing ties Quesnel, or any mill, to a Nation framework yet.

00:18:46.349 --> 00:18:51.085
<v Riley>The framework came out yesterday. Give it a season before calling it a dead letter.

00:18:51.699 --> 00:19:01.995
<v Casey>I'll give it a season. I won't give it the forestry money. The woodlot owners we mentioned earlier want a seat, and Nation-owned ventures and newcomers aren't named as a category at all.

00:19:02.621 --> 00:19:10.501
<v Riley>Asking for a seat is the first step toward getting one. A woodlot owner with fibre to sell is a supplier a fibre-short mill can use.

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:18.318
<v Casey>Asking isn't having. The allocation hasn't been written. So any condition that favours a town over an owner exists nowhere yet.

00:19:18.918 --> 00:19:32.910
<v Riley>That puts the fairness answer in the rules being drafted now. The money alone doesn't settle it. The operator who stayed through the lean years has a trained crew and the know-how that comes with it. A newcomer needs years to build both.

00:19:33.514 --> 00:19:44.530
<v Casey>Staying through the lean years earns first call. Who decides that? The people who left in the downturn don't get a vote. Neither does the skilled tradesperson who moved to Quesnel because the rent dropped.

00:19:45.042 --> 00:19:59.234
<v Riley>That tradesperson is the one who'd fill the shift the company can't staff. The fair answer is to honour the crew that stayed and build a training path so the newcomer gets in too. Paying for that is still cheaper than losing the mill.

00:19:59.832 --> 00:20:07.160
<v Casey>A training path is a line item. Who pays the trainer in year five, when the grant's gone and the mill's still idle? No budget line covers that.

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:18.272
<v Riley>Then the rules should require one. A Nation's criteria can ask what a project leaves behind in the town, and training is something a town keeps after the project moves on.

00:20:18.606 --> 00:20:22.070
<v Casey>It can ask. Whether it does depends on who's at the drafting table.

00:20:22.616 --> 00:20:36.112
<v Riley>The Quesnel swearing-in is the closest thing to a shared table we've got. Lhtako Dene and Nazko leaders were there from the first minute, and the Lhtako chief named the regional district as a partner, alongside the nations and the city.

00:20:36.418 --> 00:20:39.610
<v Casey>Good words. Words at a swearing-in don't set a training budget.

00:20:40.196 --> 00:20:48.524
<v Riley>No, they don't. But a council that heard from the nations on day one is better placed to ask for conditions than one that meets them at a permit hearing.

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:58.074
<v Casey>The council isn't the Tŝilhqot'in National Government, though. And Quesnel's problem, as the company describes it, is markets and fibre. Review rules aren't the cause.

00:20:58.639 --> 00:21:06.039
<v Riley>Then I'll narrow it. Nation-set criteria are a model for a restart that holds once it happens. They aren't a cure for pulp prices.

00:21:06.318 --> 00:21:09.750
<v Casey>Then the model is a hypothesis. Show me one mill restart that used it.

00:21:10.282 --> 00:21:22.754
<v Riley>There isn't one yet, and that's what I'd watch for. A Nation-owned operation sets its own hiring priorities. An owner far away sets them from head office. Whoever writes the rules decides who gets the shifts.

00:21:23.378 --> 00:21:37.730
<v Casey>And head office has told Quesnel employees how long the effluent system runs and nothing about a restart date. So what does a mill town get when the processes disagree? Ottawa's fast track, a Nation's review, and a company's letter all land on the same people.

00:21:38.346 --> 00:21:44.242
<v Riley>The company's letter went to employees. That's the only notice anyone has had, and it came from the company.

00:21:44.601 --> 00:21:46.977
<v Casey>And it says nothing about the people who wait.

00:21:47.589 --> 00:22:08.669
<v Riley>So that's where we are. The federal money is promised, the framework is written, and the restart rules are still being drafted. Here's what could work: give the crew that stayed first call, write a funded training path into whatever the money comes with, and make the Nation's published test something a proponent clears before breaking ground.

00:22:09.289 --> 00:22:27.305
<v Casey>Every piece of that is still unproven. The permit-before-review clause is still in the bill. The allocation is still blank. And nobody has said whose decision forestry capital waits on when a Nation and Ottawa disagree. The question about the millwright who arrived last spring stays open until someone writes it into the rules.

00:22:27.958 --> 00:22:42.070
<v Riley>If you're on that crew, or you're the newcomer, tell us how the shifts are being handed out. Write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. Somebody has to tell Quesnel where things stand, and the people living it should be heard first.

00:22:42.623 --> 00:22:47.647
<v Casey>Let's see whether the new council puts it on an agenda before the money arrives in 2027.

00:22:47.996 --> 00:22:51.964
<v Riley>That's Cariboo Signals for this Tuesday. Enjoy the rest of your day.

00:22:52.305 --> 00:22:54.697
<v Casey>And mind the drizzle on the drive into Quesnel.

00:22:55.297 --> 00:23:14.473
